## Summary

Implemented PRD-071 (Batch-Tier Independent Review) in full.

**What changed:**
- **`familiar-ai-review`**: `ReviewTier::Batch` + `ReviewTierPolicy.batch_risk_classes` (tier.rs); `ReviewCoordinator` gained an optional `batch_reviewer` field and dispatches to it only when tier selection picks Batch, falling back to the interactive reviewer otherwise (coordinator.rs); a new `ReviewExecutionError::Parked` signal lets a batch-tier `ReviewAgent` submit and return immediately — the coordinator marks `ReviewCycle.batch_pending` and returns without consuming a retry attempt or entering `stop()`; the structured-review JSON parser used by the interactive transport was extracted into a shared `parse_structured_review_text` so batch and interactive results go through identical parsing.
- **`familiar-ai-core`**: `ReviewTierPolicyConfig` gained `batch_risk_classes`, `max_batch_wait_ms`, `batch_worker`, validated at config load (risk vocabulary, positive wait bound, and cross-referenced against a `worker_registry` entry running the `anthropic-api` runtime) — batch tiering defaults off.
- **`familiar-ai-llm`**: Added the Anthropic Message Batches API wire client (`submit_message_batch`, `retrieve_message_batch`, `fetch_message_batch_results`) with wiremock tests.
- **`familiar-ai-storage`**: New `batch_reviews` table (migration 058) and `BatchReviewRepository` — idempotent submission by `review_id`, atomic exactly-once result consumption, durable expiry-fallback recording.
- **`familiar-ai-daemon`**: New `batch_review.rs` module with `BatchReviewAgent` (the `ReviewAgent` that submits/polls/parses/records accounting for the batch tier, with a synchronous interactive fallback on expiry) and a background poller (`batch_review::run`) that resumes pending batches across restarts and re-drives disposition via the existing `resume_implemented_checkpoint` path; wired into `main.rs`, `run.rs` (including the critical `cycle.batch_pending` early-return that frees the PRD's slot instead of marking it blocked), and a new `familiar-ai batch-review enable/disable/pending` CLI surface using the existing audited-config-mutation sink.
- Dedicated test file `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/tests/batch_review.rs` covers park-and-free, crash-resume idempotence, expiry fallback, single-disposition-path, and tier-partitioned accounting — all against `wiremock`, no live calls.

**Verification:** full workspace `cargo test` (all crates, all pre-existing suites) passes with zero failures; `cargo fmt --check` and `cargo clippy -D warnings` are clean except one pre-existing, unrelated `nonminimal_bool` finding in `bootstrap.rs` (confirmed via git-stash bisection, not touched by this PRD). The daemon *binary* (tray-enabled) fails to link in this sandbox only because system library `libxdo` isn't installed — confirmed pre-existing via the same stash bisection; the library and the non-tray `familiar-ai`/`familiar-ai-daemon` binaries build and test cleanly under `--no-default-features`.

One disclosed interpretation: the raw Anthropic Messages/Batches API never returns a dollar cost field (only token counts) for API-key billing, so "provider-reported cost" is recorded as a `configured-rate` local estimate (via `[execution_history.pricing]`, reusing existing PRD-051 plumbing) when a rate is configured, and left honestly unknown otherwise — never fabricated, consistent with PRD-059's established design for this exact runtime.
