This looks coherent and correctly scoped. Summary of the remediation:

**Root cause confirmed:** `familiar-ai batch-review enable` writes to `repositories.<key>.review.tier_policy`, but `batch_auth`, `build_batch_reviewer`, and the daemon's batch poller (`batch_review::run`) only ever read the top-level `config.review.tier_policy`. A repository enabled via the shipped CLI would never resolve a batch worker/credential — worse, the poller's startup gate used the same global-only check, so it would exit immediately and never poll for *any* repository's batch reviews if only a per-repository override was set.

**Fix** (entirely within `crates/familiar-ai-daemon/src/batch_review.rs` and its test file, no scope creep):
1. Added `resolved_tier_policy(config, repository_key)` — looks up `config.repositories.get(repository_key)` for a repo-scoped override first, falling back to the global `config.review.tier_policy`.
2. `batch_auth` and `build_batch_reviewer` now take/use `repository_key` through this helper instead of reading the global policy directly.
3. Replaced the poller's single-config startup gate with `any_batch_configured`, which checks both the global default and every repository override.
4. `poll_once` now resolves auth per-row via `batch_auth(config, &row.repository_key)` instead of one shared credential for every repository — correct since different repos can name different batch workers.
5. Added `build_batch_reviewer_resolves_a_repository_scoped_enablement` in `tests/batch_review.rs`, proving a repo-only enablement (global policy left `None`) resolves `Some`, and an unrelated repository correctly resolves `None`.

Verified: `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets` (0 warnings), and `cargo test -p familiar-ai-core -p familiar-ai-review -p familiar-ai-agent -p familiar-ai-storage` plus the full `familiar-ai-daemon` test suite (including the new test) all pass. No commits made.
